Record that commands sent right after a play can overtake it

A customer playing a sound and immediately setting its speed saw "Speed
command matched no active samples" on a main-era build, then found the
problem gone on v2.1 and asked whether it was really fixed. It is not.

Both lines load a play's file outside the command loop: main hands the
load to tokio::spawn, and v2.1 parks the play in the `loads` JoinSet and
re-enters it into the loop only once prepared. In both, any command that
arrives while the load is in flight runs first and matches nothing. v2.1
also logs "Processing command: Play" only after preparation, which makes
its logs look serialized, and it does not log selector misses for MQTT
senders at all, so the failure became silent rather than absent. The
customer's v2.1 excerpt shows a speed aimed at a different voice
(CrankLoop2), so it does not exercise the play-then-speed pattern.

Reproduced both builds with a local mosquitto broker and a null ALSA
sink, sending play+speed pairs for the same voice over one connection at
controlled gaps. Back-to-back pairs on a cached file drop the speed
20/20 on both lines. Around 300 us, which matches the customer's 281 us,
roughly half drop. At 1 ms both lines usually apply it, matching the
customer's "a millisecond delay worked around it" observation. Cold loads
widen the window. A voice_stop sent right after a looping play was also
confirmed to leave the loop running on v2.1.

docs/bugs.md gains an entry with the mechanism, the measurements, the
HTTP-based workaround, and the two candidate fixes. No code is changed:
the fix is a choice about ordering semantics to make together first.

